# Target C: high-t degree-three fans force a linear boundary-rhombus collar

**Date:** 2026-10-02  
**Base main:** \`3ce2297d98ac2b2a919bdf9869a06278d7b96048\`  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening in the canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container
Type-II branch. Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Statement

Work in the high-\(t\), low-outer-walk, square-root-diameter-matching regime of the
current-main theorem

\[
\texttt{research/target\_c\_high\_t\_degree3\_fan\_forcing\_2026-10-02.md}.
\]

Write

\[
t_D={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\nu=\nu(H),
\qquad
x_B=b_{\rm out}-q,
\]

and let

\[
I=U+T
\]

be the internal good-fan population, split into contact-degree-two vertices \(U\) and
contact-degree-three vertices \(T\).

Let \(N_4^{\partial}\) be the number of bounded strict unit-rhombus contact faces whose
one side is a loaded-hull good-fan edge joining two consecutive degree-three fan
vertices.

Then the finite estimate

\[
\boxed{
N_4^{\partial}
\ge
4\,\operatorname{per}(F)
-6t_DD
-6\nu
-9x_B
-8
}
\tag{1}
\]

holds.

Consequently, in the present branch

\[
\nu=O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
x_B=O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to\pi,
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
N_4^{\partial}
\ge
(4\pi-6t_D-o(1))D.
}
\tag{2}
\]

At the widest current high-\(t\) endpoint \(t_D\le1.860+o(1)\),

\[
\boxed{
N_4^{\partial}
>
(1.4063706143\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{3}
\]

Moreover, if \(N_2^{\partial}\) denotes the number of internal degree-three fan
vertices whose **both** adjacent good-fan faces are among these strict rhombi, then

\[
\boxed{
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
2N_4^{\partial}-T.
}
\tag{4}
\]

Using the current-main lower bound

\[
T\ge(2\pi-2t_D-o(1))D,
\]

together with the trivial upper bound \(T\le q=(\pi+o(1))D\) would not sharpen
(4).  Instead the finite derivation below combines (4) directly with the same
fan-count ledgers and yields, asymptotically,

\[
\boxed{
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
(6\pi-10t_D-o(1))D.
}
\tag{5}
\]

At \(t_D\le1.860+o(1)\),

\[
\boxed{
N_2^{\partial}
>
(0.2495559215\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{6}
\]

Thus the surviving high-\(t\) branch contains not merely many third-contact spokes:
it contains a linear one-rhombus-thick boundary collar, including a linear population
of exact two-cell rhombus strips.

No computation or verifier is used.

## 2. Adjacent degree-three pairs in the good fans

The current good-fan theorem partitions the good diameter leaves into at most

\[
\boxed{
R\le\nu+x_B
}
\tag{7}
\]

same-carrier fan runs.

Inside one run, record the internal fan vertices as a word in the symbols

\[
\mathrm T,\mathrm U,
\]

according as the contact degree is three or two.

If a run contains \(T_r\) symbols \(\mathrm T\) and \(U_r\) symbols \(\mathrm U\),
the number \(A_r\) of adjacent \(\mathrm{TT}\) pairs satisfies

\[
A_r\ge T_r-U_r-1.
\tag{8}
\]

Indeed the \(T_r\) symbols are split into at most \(U_r+1\) nonempty T-blocks, and a
T-block of length \(\ell\) contributes \(\ell-1\) adjacent TT pairs.

Summing (8) over all good-fan runs gives

\[
\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{TT}}
\ge T-U-R.
}
\tag{9}
\]

Every pair counted by \(A_{\mathrm{TT}}\) corresponds to one unit loaded-hull contact
edge

\[
e_i=p_ip_{i+1}
\]

between two consecutive degree-three leaves in one exact same-carrier
\((D,D,1)\) fan.

## 3. Remove bridge hull edges

Let \(J\) be the number of unit loaded-hull sides which are bridges of the positive
contact graph.

The existing exact bridge-occurrence lemma gives

\[
\boxed{
J\le x_B.
}
\tag{10}
\]

Therefore at least

\[
A_{\mathrm{TT}}-x_B
\tag{11}
\]

of the TT hull edges are nonbridges and have a unique bounded contact face on their
interior side.

Fix one such edge

\[
e=pp'.
\]

Let \(z,z'\) be the unique third-contact neighbors of \(p,p'\), respectively.
The current inward-spoke theorem gives

\[
z\ne z'
\tag{12}
\]

for consecutive leaves in one fan.

Because \(p\) has exactly the two hull fan contacts and the inward spoke \(pz\), the
bounded face incident with \(e\) must leave \(p\) through \(pz\).  Similarly it must
leave \(p'\) through \(p'z'\).  Hence its boundary has the form

\[
p\;-\;p'\;-\;z'\;-\;\mathcal P\;-\;z\;-\;p,
\tag{13}
\]

where \(\mathcal P\) is a positive-contact path from \(z'\) to \(z\).

## 4. One TT hull edge gives either defect or a strict rhombus

If \(\mathcal P\) has one edge, then

\[
zz'\in C
\]

and the face boundary is

\[
p-p'-z'-z-p.
\tag{14}
\]

All four sides have length one.

This simple equilateral quadrilateral is a parallelogram.  One direct proof uses the
diagonal \(pz'\): the two triangles \(pp'z'\) and \(pzz'\) both have side lengths

\[
1,1,|pz'|,
\]

so they are SSS-congruent.  Since \(p'\ne z\), the two circle-intersection points
lie on opposite sides of \(pz'\), which gives the parallelogram relation

\[
\boxed{
z'-z=p'-p,
\qquad
z-p=z'-p'.
}
\tag{15}
\]

The contact graph is triangle-free.  Hence neither diagonal is a unit contact:

\[
|pz'|>1,
\qquad
|p'z|>1.
\tag{16}
\]

Thus (14) is an actual **strict unit rhombus**.

If \(\mathcal P\) has at least two edges, then the bounded face has degree at least
five and is one of the exceptional faces counted by

\[
Q=\sum_G(\deg G-4).
\]

So every nonbridge TT hull edge has the exact local dichotomy

\[
\boxed{
\text{strict boundary rhombus}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\text{bounded exceptional face}.
}
\tag{17}
\]

## 5. Exceptional faces pay TT hull edges one-for-one

A priori one exceptional face might contain many TT hull edges.  Boundary-occurrence
counting removes that multiplicity.

Fix an exceptional bounded face \(G\), and let \(r_G\) be the number of nonbridge TT
hull edges assigned to \(G\).

For every assigned hull edge \(pp'\), the boundary occurrence of that hull edge is
distinct.  At its two endpoints, Section 3 shows that the boundary of \(G\) uses the
two corresponding inward-spoke occurrences \(pz\) and \(p'z'\).

These \(2r_G\) spoke **occurrences** are distinct.  The only possible physical-edge
reuse is when two adjacent assigned TT hull edges share their middle leaf \(p\) and
therefore share the physical spoke \(pz\).  If the two assigned hull edges belong to
the same face \(G\), then \(pz\) is a bridge on the boundary of \(G\) and appears
twice in the boundary walk, once for each side.  Thus the two required occurrences
remain distinct.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\deg G\ge3r_G.
}
\tag{18}
\]

If \(r_G=1\), exceptionality gives

\[
\deg G-4\ge1=r_G.
\]

If \(r_G\ge2\), (18) gives

\[
\deg G-4\ge3r_G-4\ge r_G.
\]

Therefore in all cases

\[
\boxed{
r_G\le\deg G-4.
}
\tag{19}
\]

Summing over exceptional faces,

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm exc}^{\mathrm{TT}}\le Q.
}
\tag{20}
\]

There is no whole-hull exception here: the charge uses the three specific boundary
occurrences forced by each TT edge, rather than cyclic-run endpoints.

A quadrilateral face cannot be assigned to two distinct TT hull edges, because
(18) with \(r_G=2\) would give \(\deg G\ge6\).  Hence the remaining quadrilateral
faces are distinct.

Combining (9)--(11) and (20),

\[
\boxed{
N_4^{\partial}
\ge
T-U-R-x_B-Q.
}
\tag{21}
\]

## 6. Eliminate \(U,R,Q\)

Current main gives

\[
R\le\nu+x_B,
\tag{22}
\]

and the exact degree-two fan-face payment gives

\[
U\le2x_B+Q+4.
\tag{23}
\]

Insert (22)--(23) in (21):

\[
\boxed{
N_4^{\partial}
\ge
T-\nu-4x_B-2Q-4.
}
\tag{24}
\]

The current high-\(t\) fan theorem gives the finite estimate

\[
T
\ge
2\operatorname{per}(F)
-2t_DD
-5\nu
-5x_B
-4.
\tag{25}
\]

Also the exact loaded-hull face identity

\[
b_{\rm out}+Q=2t_DD
\]

and

\[
b_{\rm out}\ge\operatorname{per}(F)
\]

give

\[
\boxed{
Q\le2t_DD-\operatorname{per}(F).
}
\tag{26}
\]

Substituting (25)--(26) into (24),

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_4^{\partial}
&\ge
2\operatorname{per}(F)-2t_DD
-6\nu-9x_B-8
-2Q\\
&\ge
4\operatorname{per}(F)
-6t_DD
-6\nu
-9x_B
-8.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (1).  Type-II disk convergence and the square-root hypotheses give
(2)--(3).

## 7. Linear population of two-cell boundary strips

Consider the graph whose vertices are the \(T\) degree-three internal good-fan
vertices and whose edges are the \(N_4^{\partial}\) forced strict-rhombus hull edges.

It is a disjoint union of paths, hence a forest.  If it has \(c\) nontrivial
components, then

\[
c\le T-N_4^{\partial},
\tag{27}
\]

because the full forest on the \(T\) vertices has \(T-N_4^{\partial}\) components
after isolated vertices are included.

A path component containing \(\ell\) selected rhombus edges contains \(\ell-1\)
vertices incident with two selected edges.  Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_2^{\partial}
&=
\sum_{\text{nontrivial components}}(\ell-1)\\
&=
N_4^{\partial}-c\\
&\ge
2N_4^{\partial}-T.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (4).

For a finite bound, combine (24) with \(T=I-U\) and the elementary
\(T\le I\le q\).  In the asymptotic high-\(t\) branch it is simpler to use

\[
{T\over D}\le{q\over D}\to\pi.
\tag{28}
\]

Then (2) and (28) give only

\[
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
(7\pi-12t_D-o(1))D,
\]

which is weaker than (5) near the live endpoint.

To retain the stronger coefficient, use the exact relation behind (24):

\[
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
2(T-U-R-x_B-Q)-T
=
T-2U-2R-2x_B-2Q.
\tag{29}
\]

Apply (22)--(23):

\[
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
T-2\nu-8x_B-4Q-8.
\tag{30}
\]

Now insert (25) and (26):

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_2^{\partial}
&\ge
2\operatorname{per}(F)-2t_DD
-7\nu-13x_B-12
-4Q\\
&\ge
6\operatorname{per}(F)-10t_DD
-7\nu-13x_B-12.
\end{aligned}
\tag{31}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
N_2^{\partial}
\ge
(6\pi-10t_D-o(1))D,
}
\]

which is (5).  At \(t_D=1.860\),

\[
6\pi-18.6
=
0.2495559215\ldots,
\]

proving (6).

## 8. Exact geometry of a two-cell strip

Take one vertex \(p_i\) counted by \(N_2^{\partial}\).  Its two neighboring fan
faces are strict rhombi

\[
p_{i-1}p_i z_i z_{i-1}
\]

and

\[
p_ip_{i+1}z_{i+1}z_i.
\]

Applying (15) to both cells gives

\[
p_{i-1}-z_{i-1}
=
p_i-z_i
=
p_{i+1}-z_{i+1}
=:v,
\qquad |v|=1,
\tag{32}
\]

and

\[
z_i-z_{i-1}=p_i-p_{i-1},
\qquad
z_{i+1}-z_i=p_{i+1}-p_i.
\tag{33}
\]

Thus the inner three-port chain is an exact translate of the outer three-leaf fan
chain:

\[
\boxed{
z_j=p_j-v,\qquad j=i-1,i,i+1.
}
\tag{34}
\]

The three ports belong to one unloaded positive-contact component because
\(z_{i-1}z_i\) and \(z_iz_{i+1}\) are unit contacts.

So (6) is not merely a face count: it gives a linear population of exact
two-rhombus harmonic collars with one common inward-spoke direction across each
two-cell strip.

## 9. Stopped-train consequence

Each forced boundary rhombus has one loaded-hull outer side.  In the strict-rhombus
train decomposition, that outer side is one terminal outer occurrence of the train
running through the rhombus in the direction of that side.

Distinct forced rhombi have distinct loaded-hull sides, hence give
\(N_4^{\partial}\) distinct outer terminal occurrences.  A complete stopped train
has at most two outer terminal occurrences.  Therefore the number
\(N_{\rm fan\text{-}train}\) of complete stopped trains meeting this boundary collar
satisfies

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm fan\text{-}train}
\ge {N_4^{\partial}\over2}.
}
\tag{35}
\]

At \(t_D\le1.860+o(1)\),

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm fan\text{-}train}
>
(0.7031853071\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{36}
\]

This does **not** claim that these trains are outer-to-outer.  A train may terminate
at a bounded exceptional face.

If \(L_{OO}^{\rm fan}\) counts only the trains in this family whose other endpoint is
also outer, the total bounded exceptional-face endpoint capacity gives the safe
refinement

\[
\boxed{
L_{OO}^{\rm fan}
\ge
{(N_4^{\partial}-(Q+4f))_+\over2}.
}
\tag{37}
\]

No positive lower bound for (37) is asserted from the current scalar region.

## 10. Frontier effect

The high-\(t\), low-\(x_B\), square-root-matching branch now simultaneously contains

\[
T\ge(2\pi-2t_D-o(1))D
\]

degree-three fan leaves and

\[
N_4^{\partial}
\ge(4\pi-6t_D-o(1))D
\]

strict rhombi directly under the loaded hull.

At \(t_D\le1.860\), this means:

- \(>2.56318D-o(D)\) degree-three exact fan terminals;
- \(>1.40637D-o(D)\) boundary-adjacent strict rhombi;
- \(>0.24955D-o(D)\) exact two-cell rhombus strips;
- \(>0.70318D-o(D)\) complete stopped trains touching the collar.

Every boundary rhombus also identifies two consecutive loaded fan attachments to the
same unloaded harmonic component, and its inner side is an actual unloaded-unloaded
unit contact.

The remaining problem is therefore no longer a diffuse attachment family.  It
contains a macroscopic exact rhombus collar coupled simultaneously to

1. the pentagon sea / exceptional-face train endpoint network;
2. the harmonic attachment components carrying the inward ports; and
3. the outer stopped-train endpoint capacity.

A closing theorem should now compare the collar count to one of those exact endpoint
ledgers.  In particular, either improve (37) to force a linear O--O subfamily, or
show that routing \(>1.406D\) collar occurrences into bounded exceptional faces
violates the Hall/rank constraints of the exceptional-face closure network.

**Disposition:** exact proof-only structural strengthening.  No numerical search,
verifier execution, CI result, or unexecuted certificate code is used.
